"<p>A server can publish its applications through a <strong>Cloudflare Tunnel</strong> instead of open public ports. Nitrobase only requires the connector token—it never needs access to your Cloudflare account API.</p>\n<h2>Setup Steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In Cloudflare Zero Trust Dashboard, go to <strong>Networks -> Tunnels -> Create a tunnel (cloudflared)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Copy the tunnel token.</li>\n<li>In Nitrobase, open <strong>Server -> Settings -> Cloudflare Tunnel</strong>. Paste the token and enable <strong>Tunnel only</strong>.</li>\n<li>In Cloudflare Dashboard, add a <strong>Public Hostname</strong> pointing to service:\n<pre class=\"shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark\" style=\"--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e\" tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"language-text\"><span class=\"line\"><span>http://nitrobase-traefik:8081</span></span></code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"docCallout docCallout-tip\"><div class=\"docCalloutHeader\"><span class=\"docCalloutBadge\">TIP</span><span class=\"docCalloutTitle\">Zero Open Ports</span></div><div class=\"docCalloutContent\">\n<p>In Tunnel-Only mode, ports 80 and 443 can be completely blocked on your server firewall. Cloudflare terminates TLS and securely tunnels requests directly to Traefik's private entrypoint.</p>\n</div></div>"